                   Vision Workbench (VW) Libraries
                   ===============================

1. About the VW libraries

This package provides a set of libraries that aid developement of computer
vision applications. The first two are always required, and the remainder give
additional functionality for capturing images, controlling bits of hardware
and using various GUIs:
  - VNL (Vision Numerics Library): Matrices, vectors and numerical algorithms
  - VW: The heart of VW - image classes, image processing and view geometry
  - X: Drawing into an X-window (2D)
  - XForms: Drawing into an XForms window (2D)
  - GTK: Provides GTK based interfaces and displays (2D and 3D)
  - GLOW: Simple interfaces and displays for the GLOW toolkit based on Glut
  - GL: 2D and 3D drawing using OpenGL
  - Wx: 2D and 3D drawing using WxWidgets
  - Firewire: Capture and control of IEEE1394 digital cameras
  - Meteor: Capture from the Matrox Meteor frame-grabber
  - Yorick: Control of the Yorick series of active stereo platforms
  - GTI: Control of the GTI mobile robot


2. Compilation

We have not fully worked out the best way to use automake in a
foolproof fashion regardless of the cionfiguration of the machine you
have.  Follow the following steps:

   Oa> remove acextra.m4 if it exists
   0b> comment out the lines in configure.ac that refer to components
you don't have on your machine (except the AM_CONDITIONAL lines).  For
example few have installed Wx, and FC3/4 don;t support GTK 1.2.

Now do the "normal" procedure:
   1> ./bootstrap
   2> ./configure
   3> make
   4> make install

Note that the last step installs include files and libraries into the
local tree in "./include" and "./lib" respectively.  Only after make
install will changes to the library affect applications that
compile/link against VW34, so it is important to remember to do this.

By default the libraries are compiled with debugging symbols, but if
you want to compile with full optimisation, use the additional
configure flags:
   ./configure CXXFLAGS=-O6 CFLAGS=-O6


3. License

Each library directory contains a file COPYING which specifies the
terms under which the library may be used and copied.


4. Documentation

This package contains documentation in source form. To create readable
documentation from these sources requires a version of the program
Doxygen, available from http://www.doxygen.Xxx/xxx/xxx. Simply type
'make docs' in this directory.


5. Maintainer

This package is maintained by the members of the Active Vision Lab,
University of Oxford:

http://www.robots.ox.ac.uk/ActiveVision
